# Patch for GitHub issue #36 — 3 confirmed bugs in src/guard/vet/external.ts
#
# Why this is a .patch file and not a commit: src/guard/** is self-policy-protected
# (defaults.ts's SELF_POLICY_PATTERN denies Edit/Write there through the harness,
# by design, so a model can't widen its own enforcement contract). This patch was
# prepared by an agent working within that same floor, so it could write the fix
# content but not apply it to the protected path directly. Apply and commit it
# yourself: `git apply patches/issue-36-vet-external-bugs.patch` from the repo root.
#
# What it fixes (see patches/issue-36-vet-external-bugs.md for the full writeup):
#   1. parseSemgrepOutput's finding `id` is now the bare rule id, not semgrep's
#      real path-prefixed check_id.
#   2. parseOsvOutput's severity now comes from a real CVSS 3.1 Base Score
#      computed from the actual vector string osv-scanner returns, so `critical`
#      is reachable again. Falls back to the source database's own curated
#      rating (`database_specific.severity`) when no CVSS vector is present.
#   3. Both exec call sites (`binaryAvailable`, the real scan invocations) now
#      pass a scrubbed copy of this process's own env (`sandbox.ts`'s existing
#      `scrubEnv`) instead of `{}`, which was breaking semgrep's Windows Python
#      entry point.
#
# Verification: typechecked in isolation against this repo's real tsconfig.base.json
# flags (strict, exactOptionalPropertyTypes, noUncheckedIndexedAccess, all clean,
# exit 0) and run for real against semgrep 1.173.0 and osv-scanner 1.9.2 on this
# machine — confirmed check_id now resolves to the bare rule id, and all 6 real
# lodash 4.17.15 findings now classify at the correct severity, cross-checked
# against osv-scanner's own `database_specific.severity` field (exact match on
# every one: 3x medium/MODERATE, 3x high/HIGH). Full log of that verification is
# in this session's transcript; not reproduced here to keep the patch focused.
#
# NOT included here: the two "documents the bug" assertions in
# test/guard/vet-external.test.ts (lines ~262-266 and ~332-335) that currently
# assert the BUGGY behavior is correct, by design, as honest documentation of a
# known-but-unfixed bug. Once this patch lands, those two specific assertions
# will fail — not a regression, just the inverse of what they were built to
# prove. A separate patch (issue-36-test-assertions.patch) updates exactly those
# two assertions to check the fixed behavior instead; nothing else in that test
# file is touched. Apply both together, or apply this one and update the test
# file yourself if you'd rather review that change independently.

